        <p>Regland, in an intellectual sense, will he all the poorer by the return of Mr. Mennen. U. Conway to America, to spend the remainder of hie life in New York. lie preached hie farewell sermon at South- place chnniii. in Finshury, on Sundwr,and bade hie congregation a touching farewell. Hia connection with the chapel him existed for a third of a cen- tury, beginning as it did an far back no Mt lie .his connection with it now chiefly owing to the illness of hie wife, who returns with him to , New York, where their children are living. Mr. IConway is one of the inlet thoughtful, syropathe• tic, and stimulating of writers ; and even in Lon- don. myriads who never heard him preach, nor knew even where he preachwl, have enjoyed his . brilliant contributions to our magssine literature. Hie literary work 11,4 not neeesearily cease with . his departure, an .1 I have no doubt, the literature of the linglishopeaking race will continue to be en- richer] by the products of his eloquent and thought- ful pen.</p>
        <p>I Writing of the recent norm on the west COM% the "Blackpool Iterate referetn the experience, of I the captain and crew of the Zillah, which were of each character that they will not readily he for- gotten. "Fur 14 bourn, roughly speaking, the neon had a continuous fiztit for exi+tence. The story,as told our representative by the planter, Captain Skelhoro, vat POOP. effect. The Zillah, which inn smell steamer belonging to the Steam Shipping Ct.,' Limited, of Warrington, was on her way to bleat- wood with a cargo of limnitone for the United Alkali Company. Besides the Captain, there were on board, the Mate, Hulse, the engineer, Sprownen ; fireman, Bent, and the conk, Harrison. The yowl arrived off F.eetweel albeit seven o'clock in the morning, hut prior Co that time she had been having n heavy time of it with the strong wind rind ens. When she arrived off Fleetwood, the wind we, boisterous, to Yen the captainle own phrase, and was blowing su. by S. It WWI seen t list the Vogel, in spite of their beet Wa4 drifting K and N. in the direction of Lune Daep. and a, the morning advanced the wind increased to a whole gale. The Vessel we, unable to make any headway against the wind and tide, and no the tog Fylle wax in the nci,thluour. hood rho was hailed for avei.tance, which was at once given. The Zillah wa, taken in tow by the rylde, and for about all hour the two kept company. Unfortunately, this. Oa Oro., of the weather, No. 4 Buoy Wee faille&amp; and in order to get cle,r it wee vow ppppp y to cant off from the Zillah. Worm, happened, however,for the tow ropebgetting entangled in the liryld.ds propeller, practically crippled that vonftelAnd eh* returned to port. The Zillah,however.wn, !triable to take advan- tage of the start oho hed got, and again hngen to drift. flee condition alOn became hopeless. The vessel wan swept by every wave, and her firni worn in a very short Brun drowned out. Sheerly Ohs struck the 'sandbank And there she r.neninel, ever, minute seeing the wave, melon" high, and higher as the tide came in. The mew mak to the rirtging —or rather the captain and with took 'o the rigging, the engineer and fireman, with greet dlfflonnY manage,' to ft uflorelt on the top of the funnel, and the cook found a perilnue footing en the top of the ventilater. While in terrible Position,the engineer hid the misfo-tuna to be precipitated to the bottom of the funnel. Init al- though severely hurt, he was able, with the Alai.- tango of the firemen, to regain his yraition. By and bye, the bat law their theribln plight, ere/</p>
        <p>Nlembiirs of Parliament: whip were at the Royal garden party describe it as listing been a great solc- 'C.+. Tho ljdoon, thy soy, wile moat kindly and gracious, awl the arrangements were excellent :The Coon official, they think have derived a Int- roit from the event on Werltnirday lent. One of the aide incidence of the gathering—an incident which attracted much interested attention—war the photographing of a group coneirting of Ntr. Cham- hyolnie. Lord Balfour of Burton:lL and the Prime Minister of Canada.</p>
        <p>The Metropolitan policemen lea proud end happy man this week. Ile has the premise of four days' pay an reward of Lie Jubilee exertions, and ho has the performance of • long-deferred pledge that he should be provided with light summer attire. Ile is on duty in the street in a feat herswitight helmet and a neat blue-serge tunic. clostily resembling the undress uniform of officers in the line regiments. It it loose without being heggyis worn without a boll, and has an outside pocket on each hroset,ffited with buttoned flaps. It is • very grateful change from the heavy tunic of blue cloth end the tight loather belt.</p>
        <p>There was a pleasant martial gathering of trade union leaders on Monday night at T tynbee whore they met, at the Invitation of Canon Barnett and the residents at the eettlenient, to Aug and dis- . cu-s the progress of the industrial movement dur- ing the past sixty years. After supper Canon Barnett introduced the talking by a genial little epoch in which he offered a welcome to the guesta and pointed out the appropriateness of curb gathering in that place, inasmuch an Teynbee flail represented an attempt to bring about closer re- Winne between Learning and Labour. Professor 13. Beenly proposed e Prosperity to the Trade Union Movement," referring especially to the ehar- acterieticti of the period extending from 1860 to 1875, ending with the winning of that piece of legis- lation which had over since been rightly regarded as the charter of trade unioniem.Mr. sydney Webb, in seconding the toast, said the most important point to consider at the present, time war the legal position of trade unionism. There MU &lt;leery in- dication that the deciaion of the 11011.1 of Lord, in the case of Flood v. Allen would be adverse to the trade unions, and that tile triumph won in 18Th would consequently be undone. That, would mean that they would have to embark et once upon a campaign for a new Act of Parliament which ehould definitely tell the judge, and the Ronal of tor la what Parliament believed it had told them in 1875.</p>
        <p>The prockedinge in Parliament are seldom as comic as they were on Monday night. That the , strongest Ministry of modern time, ahould be de- feated three times in one evening,and that • Minis- terial !Sill, dealing, of all things, with the Church. should have actually bran killed under the eyek of the icader of the House, is abeurd enough. But what makes it rather worse is that it all arena MP of • garden party at the very Palace where Mem- bers were treated so contumeliously only last week. The stoutest Tories wen., we onderktand, loudest in I heir wrath at the " insult " they sot • fared when they were last at Buckingham Palace. Yet they wore no anxious to Rat hack to wipe it out litre, and champagne that the Governmenk whip. could do nothing with thain,and were left lea mis- erable minority for hours together.</p>
        <p>As soon an he had • majority, Mr. Balfour promptly moved that they should all go home. tierew were very pertinent appeal. that he should I use the chance to make some progress with the I Boilers Hill, which propoQes a much needed protec- tion for a large section of rho workers. The Bill I may not be perfect, but that was all the more reason for proceeding with the Crinmitteo stage. Mr Lloyd George vainly observed that they had just had a holiday, and ought really to be lit to do some work. Pot the Tories had evidently been brought ' back from junketing only on the assurance that . they should he free to go junketing again; and : therefore the boilers must go on exploding for the I present. . .</p>
        <p>r-iiii;Xesult of the garden party—in addition to the defeats of the liovernment—wws to lead to the poss.- ponensent of the intended westing of thin Colcnixl party. The Prime Minister of Canada wan to have been pc sent to deliver an address, but he found himself "commanded" to attend the party. Some of the menthes* of the Colonial party were also desirous of obeying commands in the matter, and as a result the meeting was pwtponed until Monday next. _</p>
        <p>—II-appears on the face of it rather ungracious to grumble at the hospitality offered by the Govern- ment to members of Parliament at th s Naval Re- view but there is really good reason for the Com- ist s to woich Mt Cherie+ Shaw gone exprei4ion in his unanswered gusetion to Arlieschen on Mon• i day night. The hospitality which members and their friend, found on the Campania was provided. not by the Admimlty. but by hit John Burns and it was something like an abuse of that hospitality for the Admiralty to turn two Pie Campania at the last moment nearly a thousand ladies and gentle- men whomV was never trttoi,to,l to accommo late lon the ship at all. One thousand o°ol4 have men the deist comfortably from her deckn. hut eighteen hundred converted what should hare been a pleas- , ant excursion into an uncomf3rtehle crush. in , which ladies had to stand loran hour or two to- , get her. Following upon the Wiliness of the Ad- , miraity iu providing for the accommodation of the colonial troops m the review, the bungling of the , Campanile trip ban hardly redeemed the credit of the Admiralty staff.</p>
        <p>Sir Wilfrid Laurier in tho most enviable of Colonial visitant, for all hit improssions of London its first impressions, in his long list of banquets land luncheon* doss not blow,. him. Two things ha, particularly impressed the Liberal Premiss of Csoada --th.c3rdiality of CIA English people, for which his French traditions bad not prepared him and the freshness and sins of the London parks.</p>
        <p>Litereture, art, and law have Wei indifferently in connection with the Jubile. honours, and pre- sent a sorry retort when compered with the poli- ticians and contributor. Ni Tory polities] funds. But much surprise in now being expressed that no honour whttever has been ennferred upon any Eng.. Itch women: perhaps iii. all tho hotel% if the* noise. lion was not whim, been more disprinitnating than ' that which has been shown in the e-lection of men. With a woman upon the throne, with a Jubilee be- ing held it recognition of that • to). lady's long and dietinguishe4 reign, with 10n..; roll of Pfineonten• of thp floyal Bowie living urn +nu lio,and with many women distinguiehrd in lit..reure and a perfect . . . .</p>
