      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-07-02_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ANNUAL C MFERENCIi AT WARRINUTOY.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL C MFERENCIi AT WARRINUTOY.</p>
        <p>From Saturday until Tuesday delegates from veriona parts of the country representing the United Free Gospel and Independent Methodist Churches assembled in conference at Warrington. This we. the Sited annual meeting of the denomi- nation, and it wa+ but Skiing I hat it sikk.kki'd be held irk Warrington, when Friary' Green Church is cele. bracing its centenary. The delegates commenced to arrive on Sat urday afternoon. They were re- ceived at Friars' Green Chapel, whore tea war pro- •ided. Abont ITO delegates were regent and they were welcomed by Messrs. larding. if cnohnli. Evans, G. Mouttlield, and others. A band meet- ing was held in the chapel at seven o'clock, and tile! proceedings throughout were very enthusiastic. Mr. ! Evans conducted, assisted by Mr. Crumblehulme of Bolton, and Mr. Lee, of w igen. SUNDAYS SERVICES. On Sunday, the pulpit. of various Nonconformiet plackka of worship, wear-occupied by dele2atkeo to the Conference. Thers wore in addition,. number of open-air meetings. During the day well-attended meetings were held at Bridge Foot, Rodney Brow, Fair Ground, Brick-street, and Sankey Green.</p>
        <p>The PI...NT nf the Connexion i Mr. William Brim,- low, me- J.r. occ,spied the pulpit at the W ycliffe Congregn- Gone! Church in the morning. Flo bawl tor discourse lOn the words And they that worship lion mut woo- ehip Ilim in *pint and In truth." It war, he remarked, often said that man could no in we digress.. himself from the tendency to worship that wan in him than he could di raver himself from other thinge to which by nature how.! predinposed. A 11140 moat worship Warne it wan hi* nature to do so, and that tendency wan believed to he universally applied to mankind. They were told that no rare and no tribe of people had been hound any- where who illi not more or lent manifest the tendenc y no .anmliip, and if on occanions they met with a lack or absence of that denim such canes were only to le. regarded like t we of rusons who were burn blind or deaf, or nubjects to some other pvhslcal There seer, of course, excentinne to the rule, the rule boing tint man would worship, the object of worahip was different—different In dill v- ent countries, different in different PVF, of mankind 'rhey bad only to turn over thelrnooaotO.riptorn, or to refreeli their memories by reading, to reenewte that proposition. 'rhe hea, hens worshipped male with their own hands. The Pagans found &amp;ibex In the nun, In lire, and in other elements of mann, The Egyptians ssornhipped certain enirmale, even rearming things they were told, while Purges, philonophem and thonphtlil men of every ago and of every mntry hrd bowed awn in reverent devotion over the deep 'nye, rim ni nature. Hut in all thew varietie, there was a tendency, admire, nn aptitude and the capacity for wonhip. The question wan sot •I Will ',inn worship ? " that was retied hy history aol by human nettle, The ques.inn wan'' What will he worship and how will he worship " There were many 1111wiRe and cried methods of worship, NI the tost Impelled the toe nbject, which wan the worship of God, and an to character nod mtn• net it must be in spirit and In truth. Worship wn• not • meter of tune and place ; it was the offering of the soul 0,0 tiod anywhere whether on set or mountain, in the trackloan forest or on the burning mind, in the cot- tage or mansion, in the garret nr in the cellar —wheveyer a man lifted up his face to his Maker in apirit and in truth that wee a plain where worship inielit he offered and he accepted. True worship was spiritnal, toil its temple was the human rioul. Although the spirit in MA. arta a profound myntery, they felt, knew and reeognizel that there was. spirit in him. A npuit wan not a rinterial ouhatnuce or • physical force. Worahip rll.lO not be merely professional or ceremonial. They needed to guard against mere ceremoniala in this day, and ermine the sutetitullon nil menhanical MOM, worship by 'lent an,l ehnilancen en nubo f0r...0w/thin in spirit and in truth ; the" dim religious light," the as,- vices whore 'nevem,' display deeply imnressed the nem., Gregorian music, priestly drew .1 stately mosementa—these might he aids to devotion, or they might be hindrances. They might oven ettraet the at- tention rA the emit from the true worship of GA. NI, F. YVirsivairit, Phi I. preirehed in Wycliffe C gregatinnsil Church at night. Ile 11.4 A his ,101^^01., on the teat" h00t,,,' Allan.. and Pimp., risers of Gams.- eat better than all the waters of Grind ?" II limo, the. .</p>
